    "text": "Protection of Alcohols"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "c6144e93-2e91-4217-9f8e-a13133b04c6e"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00207:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00207:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "3-bromopropan-1-ol (hydroxy hydrogen labeled acidic) does not react with magnesium and ether to form (3-hydroxypropyl)magnesium bromide."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_17_08_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00207:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00207:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00207:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "An alcohol reacts with chlorotrimethylsilane to form trimethylsilyl ether. For example cyclohexanol reacts with chlorotrimethylsilane and triethylamine to form cyclohexyl trimethylsilyl ether with 94 percent yield."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_17_08_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00207:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00207:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "Ball-and-stick model of chlorotrimethylsilane and tertiary-butyl chloride. Carbon is more hindered for tertiary-butyl chloride and has shorter bonds. Silicon is less hindered for chlorotrimethylsilane and bonds are longer."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_17_08_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00207:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00207:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "Cyclohexyltrimethylsillyl ether reacts with hydronium ion to form cyclohexanol and trimethylsilanol."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_17_08_004.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00006",
      "segmentId": "m00207:para:para-00006"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00207:caption:fig-00005-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00207:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "Different steps are involved in using a protecting group in Grignard reaction. The steps include protecting alcohol, forming Grignard reagent, performing Grignard reaction, and removing protecting group."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_17_08_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
